q('A young adult has upper-lobe bronchiectasis, chronic sinus disease, pancreatic insufficiency and obstructive azoospermia. Which diagnosis should be specifically investigated?', 'Cystic fibrosis',[
 ('Isolated postinfectious bronchiectasis as a complete explanation','It does not readily explain the pancreatic and reproductive phenotype.'),('Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis','The airway and extrapulmonary pattern is different.'),('Primary ciliary dyskinesia as the only possibility','Ciliary disease can cause airway and reproductive symptoms, but pancreatic insufficiency strongly raises CF.')],
 'Adult presentation does not exclude CF', '**Multiorgan CFTR dysfunction can be recognized after childhood.**',['The respiratory phenotype can be variable.','Congenital absence of the vas deferens is an important clue.'], '**Use sweat chloride and appropriate genetic or functional assessment.**',['A limited mutation panel can miss uncommon variants.','Refer a suggestive phenotype to a CF service even when initial tests are inconclusive.'],[['Airway','Bronchiectasis and sinus disease'],['Pancreas','Exocrine insufficiency'],['Reproductive tract','Obstructive azoospermia']],[CF],style='clinical_scenario')
q('In a patient with a compatible CF phenotype, a properly performed sweat chloride concentration of 72 mmol/L has which interpretation?', 'It is in the diagnostic range for CF and requires appropriate confirmation and specialist integration',[
 ('It is normal because the patient is an adult','The adult phenotype does not make this a normal value.'),('It is in the intermediate 30–59 mmol/L range','72 exceeds that interval.'),('It proves the exact CFTR genotype','Sweat chloride measures functional consequence, not the specific sequence variants.')],
 'Sweat chloride is a functional diagnostic test', '**A value at least 60 mmol/L supports CF diagnosis in the appropriate clinical setting.**',['Testing quality and confirmation matter.','Genotyping remains important for counseling and treatment eligibility.'], '**Do not replace chloride measurement with an unrelated sweat metric.**',['Conductivity alone is not an interchangeable diagnostic threshold.','Interpret the result within a complete CF assessment.'],[['Below 30','CF less likely'],['30–59','Intermediate'],['At least 60','Diagnostic range in the appropriate setting']],[CF],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A patient with suggestive bronchiectasis has sweat chloride values of 42 and 46 mmol/L on separate tests. What is the best next step?', 'Extended CFTR genetic and/or functional evaluation through a specialist service',[
 ('Exclude CF permanently because neither value reaches 60','Intermediate results can occur in CF.'),('Diagnose CF solely from a single common carrier variant','One variant may indicate carrier status and does not alone establish the full diagnosis.'),('Ignore the phenotype because sweat testing is always binary','The intermediate category requires further evaluation.')],
 'Intermediate sweat values are a diagnostic problem to resolve', '**The 30–59 mmol/L interval is not a definitive exclusion.**',['Review the clinical phenotype and test quality.','More comprehensive genetic analysis may identify missed variants.'], '**Use validated functional testing when needed.**',['Specialist nasal potential difference or intestinal-current testing can contribute.','Differentiate CF, CFTR-related disorder and alternative causes.'],[['Result','Intermediate on repeated testing'],['Next step','Extended genetic / functional assessment'],['Outcome','Integrate phenotype and CFTR evidence']],[CF],style='clinical_scenario')
q('What is the principal airway consequence of CFTR dysfunction in CF?', 'Abnormal epithelial ion transport with dehydrated secretions and impaired mucociliary clearance',[
 ('Primary destruction of pulmonary arteries without airway involvement','That is not the central CF airway mechanism.'),('Complete absence of airway neutrophils','Neutrophilic inflammation is prominent in CF lung disease.'),('Excess normal mucus clearance that prevents infection','Impaired clearance promotes infection rather than preventing it.')],
 'Ion transport abnormalities initiate a cycle of mucus retention and injury', '**CFTR dysfunction alters chloride/bicarbonate transport and airway-surface conditions.**',['Retained secretions favor infection.','Inflammation and structural damage then reinforce impaired clearance.'], '**Treat different parts of the cycle.**',['Clearance, mucoactive therapy and antimicrobials remain relevant.','Eligible CFTR modulators address an upstream molecular defect.'],[['Upstream','CFTR dysfunction'],['Airway effect','Abnormal secretions and clearance'],['Downstream','Infection, inflammation and bronchiectasis']],[CF,MU])
q('How does dornase alfa reduce sputum viscosity in CF?', 'It cleaves extracellular DNA in airway secretions',[
 ('It directly corrects the CFTR gene sequence','It is a downstream mucoactive treatment, not gene editing.'),('It inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis','It is not an antibiotic.'),('It blocks IgE binding','That is a different immunological pathway.')],
 'Neutrophil-rich secretions contain viscous extracellular DNA', '**Recombinant DNase breaks down this DNA component.**',['Reduced viscosity can aid clearance.','Its evidence in CF must not be automatically extrapolated to non-CF bronchiectasis.'], '**Use it within the prescribed airway regimen.**',['Adherence, technique and timing affect practicality.','Continue monitoring the overall pulmonary response.'],[['Substrate','Extracellular DNA'],['Action','Enzymatic cleavage'],['Role','CF mucoactive treatment']],[MU])
q('Which mechanism explains the use of inhaled hypertonic saline in selected patients with CF?', 'Osmotic hydration of airway secretions to assist mucus clearance',[
 ('Direct CFTR gene replacement','Hypertonic saline does not replace the gene.'),('Systemic suppression of all neutrophil production','It is not a systemic marrow-suppressing treatment.'),('Permanent sterilization of airway bacteria','It is not a substitute for infection-directed therapy.')],
 'Hydrating secretions can complement mechanical clearance', '**Hypertonic saline draws water into airway-surface secretions.**',['The treatment can provoke cough or bronchospasm.','Initial tolerance assessment and bronchodilator use may be appropriate.'], '**Individualize the treatment burden and response assessment.**',['It is one component of a broader CF regimen.','Do not assume every inhaled solution has the same concentration or safety profile.'],[['Mechanism','Osmotic hydration'],['Goal','Improve clearance'],['Precaution','Tolerance and bronchospasm']],[MU])
q('A patient with CF has steatorrhea, weight loss and exocrine pancreatic insufficiency. Which treatment is central?', 'Pancreatic enzyme replacement taken with meals and snacks, with nutritional assessment',[
 ('Avoid all dietary fat permanently without replacement','This can worsen caloric and nutritional deficits.'),('Use systemic corticosteroids as the enzyme substitute','They do not replace digestive enzymes.'),('Take enzymes only once weekly regardless of eating','Enzymes must be coordinated with food intake.')],
 'Maldigestion is a treatable contributor to CF morbidity', '**PERT supplies missing digestive enzyme activity.**',['Assess weight, stool symptoms and fat-soluble vitamin status.','Dose and timing are individualized.'], '**Investigate persistent symptoms before unlimited dose escalation.**',['Adherence, acid environment and alternative intestinal causes can matter.','Excessive enzyme exposure carries risks including fibrosing colonopathy.'],[['Problem','Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency'],['Treatment','PERT with food'],['Monitoring','Nutrition, symptoms and dose safety']],[EN],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Under CF Foundation guidance, which screening approach is recommended for CF-related diabetes from age 10?', 'Annual oral glucose tolerance testing',[
 ('HbA1c alone as a universally equivalent exclusion test','HbA1c can miss clinically relevant CF dysglycemia under this framework.'),('No screening until classic polyuria appears','CF-related diabetes can be clinically subtle.'),('A urine glucose dipstick once in adulthood','This is not the recommended screening strategy.')],
 'CF-related diabetes can affect nutrition and lung outcomes before classic symptoms', '**The CF Foundation recommends annual OGTT from age ten.**',['This question names that guidance because screening frameworks may differ.','Acute illness, steroid exposure and enteral feeding may require additional assessment.'], '**Use insulin when diabetes treatment is indicated under the CF care plan.**',['Coordinate nutritional and endocrine management.','Do not apply a calorie-restriction strategy reflexively to an undernourished patient.'],[['Screening test','OGTT'],['Timing','Annually from age 10 under CFF guidance'],['Care','CF and diabetes teams together']],[DM])
q('Which statement about CFTR modulator selection is correct?', 'Eligibility depends on the specific drug label, age and responsive CFTR genotype or supported functional evidence',[
 ('Every modulator works for every CFTR variant','Mechanisms and variant responsiveness differ.'),('A response permits immediate unsupervised cessation of all other CF care','Treatment changes need individualized review.'),('A sweat chloride result alone identifies the exact best drug for every patient','Genotype, label and clinical factors remain important.')],
 'Modulators are mechanism- and variant-dependent treatments', '**Correctors and potentiators address different aspects of CFTR dysfunction.**',['A combination can improve processing and channel function in eligible disease.','The set of eligible variants can evolve with labeling.'], '**Check the current local product information.**',['Review interactions, age restrictions and organ-function precautions.','Assess benefit without assuming complete anatomical reversal.'],[['Corrector','Improves processing / trafficking for responsive defects'],['Potentiator','Improves channel activity'],['Selection','Specific label and patient eligibility']],[CF,FD])
q('Which safety issue is particularly important with newer triple CFTR modulator combinations such as vanzacaftor/tezacaftor/deutivacaftor?', 'Drug-induced liver injury requiring baseline and scheduled follow-up liver testing',[
 ('No liver monitoring is needed because the treatment targets a channel','Targeted mechanism does not eliminate hepatic toxicity.'),('A normal baseline ALT guarantees no subsequent injury','Injury can emerge after treatment begins.'),('Only a complete blood count can detect the principal warning','Liver enzymes and bilirubin are central to the warning assessment.')],
 'Disease-modifying benefit must be paired with safety monitoring', '**The US label carries a drug-induced liver injury and liver failure warning.**',['Assess liver tests before initiation and according to the specified follow-up schedule.','More frequent testing may be needed with baseline abnormalities or liver disease.'], '**Respond to clinically significant abnormalities or symptoms.**',['Review interacting medicines and liver impairment.','Interrupt and evaluate treatment when labeling criteria or clinical concern warrant it.'],[['Baseline','Liver tests and risk review'],['During treatment','Scheduled liver monitoring'],['Warning signs','Clinical or biochemical liver injury']],[FD])
q('A person with CF taking chronic azithromycin has a new positive NTM sputum culture. What is appropriate while evaluating for NTM pulmonary disease?', 'Withhold azithromycin monotherapy during the evaluation to reduce resistance-selection risk',[
 ('Continue azithromycin alone as definitive NTM treatment','Single-agent macrolide exposure can select resistance.'),('Diagnose active NTM disease from one culture without clinical correlation','Disease criteria integrate symptoms, imaging and microbiology.'),('Assume all NTM are ordinary contaminants that never cause CF disease','Clinically important NTM disease occurs in CF.')],
 'A new isolate changes the macrolide safety assessment', '**CF guidance advises not continuing azithromycin during evaluation after a positive NTM culture.**',['Repeat and identify isolates appropriately.','Review symptoms and CT findings as well as competing causes.'], '**If disease is established, use a species-specific multidrug plan.**',['Susceptibility information is particularly important for key drugs.','Specialist management balances prolonged treatment benefits and toxicity.'],[['New positive culture','Evaluate for disease'],['Chronic azithromycin','Withhold during assessment'],['Confirmed disease','Species-specific combination treatment']],[NT],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Why does one NTM-positive sputum culture not automatically establish NTM pulmonary disease?', 'Environmental organisms can be isolated without progressive disease, so clinical, imaging and microbiological criteria must be integrated',[
 ('NTM never cause lung disease','They can cause important chronic pulmonary infection.'),('Only the smear result determines disease','Species, repeated cultures and the clinical picture matter.'),('Every species has identical pathogenicity and treatment','Pathogenicity and resistance vary substantially.')],
 'Isolation and disease are distinct concepts', '**Assess symptoms and compatible structural or radiological findings.**',['Repeated sputum isolation of the same species often contributes to microbiological criteria.','A bronchoscopic or biopsy result can meet a different microbiological route in the right context.'], '**A diagnosis still requires an individualized treatment decision.**',['Progression, organism and patient priorities influence whether and when to treat.','Avoid casual macrolide monotherapy when clinically relevant disease is possible.'],[['Isolation','Organism detected'],['Disease','Integrated criteria'],['Treatment','Individual benefit–risk decision']],[NT,BR])

